window.__labLoaded({"id":"6.7","title":"The Paired t-Test","goal":"Test whether a drug lowers blood pressure in the lesson's 8 patients measured before and after, with a paired t-test.","question":"A clinic measures the blood pressure of 8 patients before and after they start a new drug. Every patient's reading went down, yet the patients differ a lot from one another (readings from 129 to 161). Is the average drop of about 6 mmHg real evidence that blood pressure changed, or could it be chance?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the paired data","text":"H0: the average change in blood pressure is 0. H1: the average change is not 0. Each patient appears in both lists in the same order, so each 'after' belongs to one 'before'. Each patient's difference is their own change.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nbefore = np.array([142, 138, 155, 129, 147, 161, 133, 145])\nafter = np.array([135, 134, 148, 126, 139, 152, 130, 137])\ndiff = before - after\n\nprint('differences:', diff)\nprint(f'mean difference = {diff.mean():.3f}')\nprint(f'sd of differences = {diff.std(ddof=1):.3f}')","explain":["Load NumPy for arrays and arithmetic, under the short name np.","Load SciPy's statistics tools; the paired t-test lives in stats.","Store the 8 'before' readings. The order matters: patient 1 is first in both lists.","Store the 8 'after' readings in the same patient order, so the pairs line up.","Subtract to get each patient's own change. The paired test works on these differences, not on the two lists.","Print the 8 differences to check that they all look sensible.","Print the average drop, the centre of the differences.","Print the SD of the differences (ddof=1): how much the drops vary from patient to patient."],"meaning":"Every difference is positive (7, 4, 7, 3, 8, 9, 3, 8), so all eight patients improved. The average drop is 6.125 mmHg and the drops vary by only 2.416. The drops are steady even though the patients' blood pressures differ widely. That steadiness is what the paired test uses.","out":"differences: [7 4 7 3 8 9 3 8]\nmean difference = 6.125\nsd of differences = 2.416","figs":[]},{"title":"Run the paired t-test","text":"ttest_rel compares before and after pair by pair. A positive t means the 'before' values were higher. We also add a 95% confidence interval for the average drop.","code":"paired = stats.ttest_rel(before, after)\nci = paired.confidence_interval(confidence_level=0.95)\n\nprint(f't  = {paired.statistic:.3f}')\nprint(f'df = {paired.df}')\nprint(f'p  = {paired.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint(f'95% CI for the drop: ({ci.low:.2f}, {ci.high:.2f})')","explain":["ttest_rel is the paired t-test: it tests whether the mean of before-minus-after differences is 0.","Ask the result for a 95% confidence interval for the average drop.","Print t, the average drop measured in standard errors. Positive means 'before' was higher.","Print the degrees of freedom: 8 pairs minus 1 = 7.","Print the two-sided p-value to 4 decimals.","Print the interval for the true average drop, rounded to 2 decimals."],"meaning":"t = 7.169 with df = 7 and p of about 0.0002. If the drug changed nothing, an average change this large would almost never occur, so we reject H0. The 95% interval says the true average drop is plausibly 4.10 to 8.15 mmHg. The p-value alone would not tell us how large that is.","out":"t  = 7.169\ndf = 7\np  = 0.0002\n95% CI for the drop: (4.10, 8.15)","figs":[]},{"title":"Same result as a one-sample test","text":"A paired t-test is just a one-sample t-test on the differences, testing whether their mean is 0. The numbers come out exactly the same.","code":"one_sample = stats.ttest_1samp(diff, popmean=0)\n\nprint(f't  = {one_sample.statistic:.3f}')\nprint(f'p  = {one_sample.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint('same t as the paired test?', np.isclose(one_sample.statistic, paired.statistic))","explain":["Run an ordinary one-sample t-test on the differences, testing whether their mean is 0.","Print t from this version of the test.","Print p from this version of the test.","Check the two t values match. np.isclose compares numbers while ignoring tiny rounding noise."],"meaning":"The one-sample test on the differences gives t = 7.169, the same as the paired test, and the check prints True. A paired t-test is simply a one-sample t-test on the differences, which is why paired designs behave like single-sample problems.","out":"t  = 7.169\np  = 0.0002\nsame t as the paired test? True","figs":[]},{"title":"What the wrong test says","text":"If we ignore the pairing and treat the 16 numbers as two independent groups, the big differences between patients swamp the 6 mmHg drop. The result is far less convincing.","code":"wrong = stats.ttest_ind(before, after, equal_var=False)\n\nprint(f'independent test: t = {wrong.statistic:.2f}, p = {wrong.pvalue:.3f}')\nprint(f'paired test:      t = {paired.statistic:.2f}, p = {paired.pvalue:.4f}')","explain":["Deliberately use the WRONG test: treat before and after as two independent groups of 8 people.","Print the independent test's t and p, to see what ignoring the pairing does.","Print the correct paired result next to it for comparison."],"meaning":"Treating the readings as independent gives t = 1.25 and p = 0.231, which is not significant. The paired test gives t = 7.17 and p = 0.0002. Same numbers, opposite conclusions. Differences between patients swamp the 6 mmHg drop unless the pairing is used. Choose the test from how the data were collected.","out":"independent test: t = 1.25, p = 0.231\npaired test:      t = 7.17, p = 0.0002","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"before <- c(142, 138, 155, 129, 147, 161, 133, 145)\nafter  <- c(135, 134, 148, 126, 139, 152, 130, 137)\n\nt.test(before, after, paired = TRUE)\nt.test(before - after, mu = 0)    # same thing: one-sample test on the differences","explain":["Store the 'before' readings for the 8 patients.","Store the 'after' readings in the same patient order, so pairs match.","paired = TRUE makes t.test work on before minus after pair by pair. It prints t, df, p and a 95% interval.","The same test as a one-sample test on the differences against 0; it gives the same numbers, which shows what 'paired' means."],"expect":"t = 7.1692, df = 7, p-value = 0.0001823; 95 percent confidence interval: 4.105 to 8.145; mean difference 6.125","read":"Look at t = 7.1692, df = 7, p-value = 0.0001823: far below 0.05, so reject H0. The 95 percent confidence interval, 4.105 to 8.145, is the plausible size of the average drop, and the mean difference is 6.125. The second call repeats the same numbers.","note":"paired = TRUE keeps the two vectors in matching order. Both calls give the same t, df, p-value and confidence interval."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Compare Means > Paired-Samples T Test","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / before after.\nBEGIN DATA\n142 135 138 134 155 148 129 126\n147 139 161 152 133 130 145 137\nEND DATA.\nT-TEST PAIRS=before WITH after (PAIRED)\n  /CRITERIA=CI(.95).","explain":["Define two variables, before and after, read in free format.","Marks the start of the typed-in data.","First four patients: before and after pairs read in order (142 135, 138 134, 155 148, 129 126).","Last four patients: 147 139, 161 152, 133 130, 145 137.","Marks the end of the data.","T-TEST PAIRS=before WITH after (PAIRED) runs the paired t-test, comparing each patient's before with their own after.","/CRITERIA=CI(.95) asks for a 95% confidence interval. The period ends the command."],"read":"In the Paired Samples Test table the Mean is 6.125 (before minus after). Read t = 7.169, df = 7 and Sig. (2-tailed) = 0.000 (really 0.0002). The 95% interval 4.105 to 8.145 is for the average drop. We reject H0.","note":"In the Paired Samples Test table, the mean is before minus after (6.125). Read t, df and Sig. (2-tailed) from the same row."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput before after\n142 135\n138 134\n155 148\n129 126\n147 139\n161 152\n133 130\n145 137\nend\nttest before == after","explain":["clear removes any data in memory so we start fresh.","input starts typing data directly; before and after are the two variables, one patient per line.","Patient 1: before 142, after 135.","Patient 2: before 138, after 134.","Patient 3: before 155, after 148.","Patient 4: before 129, after 126.","Patient 5: before 147, after 139.","Patient 6: before 161, after 152.","Patient 7: before 133, after 130.","Patient 8: before 145, after 137.","end finishes data entry.","ttest before == after runs the paired t-test of the two variables, patient by patient."],"read":"In the output look at the diff row: mean 6.125 with its 95% interval (4.105 to 8.145). Then t = 7.1692 with 7 degrees of freedom and the middle line Pr(|T| > |t|) = 0.0002, the two-sided p-value. Below 0.05, so reject H0.","note":"With two variables joined by ==, ttest does a paired test by default. Read the difference row and the two-sided Pr(|T| > |t|) value."},"conclusion":"On average, blood pressure fell by 6.1 mmHg after the drug (paired t = 7.169, df = 7, p = 0.0002; 95% CI 4.10 to 8.15), and every patient improved. Caution: with no control group we cannot say the drug caused the drop (rest, time or being measured may also matter), and eight patients is a small sample.","practice":{"scenario":"A company runs a step challenge. It records the average daily steps of 10 employees in the month before the challenge and in the month during it. Did the challenge change average steps? Use a paired t-test, and compare with what the wrong (independent) test would say.","data":"before = [9024, 7900, 9000, 10157, 6986, 8304, 7305, 8190, 6219, 7131]\nafter = [9445, 8759, 9958, 10128, 7168, 9063, 7008, 8505, 6680, 8133]","tasks":["Compute each employee's change (after minus before), then the mean and SD of the changes.","Run the paired t-test and report t, df and p.","Give the 95% confidence interval for the average change.","Run the independent (Welch) test on the same numbers and explain why it disagrees."],"hint":"Use stats.ttest_rel(after, before) for the paired test and .confidence_interval(0.95). The independent version is stats.ttest_ind(after, before, equal_var=False).","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nbefore = np.array([9024, 7900, 9000, 10157, 6986, 8304, 7305, 8190, 6219, 7131])\nafter = np.array([9445, 8759, 9958, 10128, 7168, 9063, 7008, 8505, 6680, 8133])\ngain = after - before\nres = stats.ttest_rel(after, before)\nci = res.confidence_interval(0.95)\nprint('changes:', gain)\nprint(f'mean change = {gain.mean():.1f}, sd = {gain.std(ddof=1):.1f}')\nprint(f'paired t = {res.statistic:.3f}, df = {res.df}, p = {res.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint(f'95% CI: ({ci.low:.0f}, {ci.high:.0f})')\nind = stats.ttest_ind(after, before, equal_var=False)\nprint(f'wrong independent test: t = {ind.statistic:.2f}, p = {ind.pvalue:.3f}')","answer":"Steps rose by 463.1 on average (SD of changes 434.9). The paired test gives t = 3.367, df = 9, p = 0.0083, and the 95% interval is 152 to 774 extra steps a day, so we reject H0. The independent test gives t = 0.87, p = 0.398 because the large differences between people hide the gain. Caution: with no control group we cannot say the challenge caused the rise.","out":"changes: [ 421  859  958  -29  182  759 -297  315  461 1002]\nmean change = 463.1, sd = 434.9\npaired t = 3.367, df = 9, p = 0.0083\n95% CI: (152, 774)\nwrong independent test: t = 0.87, p = 0.398","figs":[]}}});
